east one prediction token\" suggests if tokens is 0, we may not have a call, but we can assume tokens >=1 from data. We'll use max(tokens, 1) to avoid division by zero.\n\nAlso note: The case has a \"threshold\" key. We'll use that.\n\nOutput must be JSON-serializable. So return a dict.\n\nImplementation: def solve(case): threshold = case['threshold']; calls = case['calls']; accepted = []; diagnostics = []; for call in calls: gain = call['loss_without_call'] - call['loss_with_call']; norm_gain = gain / max(call['prediction_tokens'], 1); if call['parseable'] and norm_gain >= threshold: accepted.append(call['call_id']); diagnostics.append(round(norm_gain, 6)); // maybe also include other? Just norm_gain. But then diagnostics list might be insufficient to map to calls. But they want per-call diagnostics, and order is preserved, so a list of floats suffices. However, accepted IDs are separate. That seems plausible.\n\nWe must not mutate case. So we will not modify case dict.\n\nReturn {\"accepted\": accepted, \"diagnostics\": diagnostics}\n\nNow encode in JSON response as specified.\n\nThe response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a JSON string. So we need to write the Python code as a string, escape newlines etc.\n\nLet me write the code:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    threshold = case['threshold']\n    calls = case['calls']\n    accepted = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for call in calls:\n        gain = call['loss_without_call'] - call['loss_with_call']\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\n        normalized_gain = gain / max(tokens, 1)\n        diagnostics.append(round(normalized_gain, 6))\n        if call['parseable'] and normalized_gain >= threshold:\n            accepted.append(call['call_id'])\n    return {'accepted': accepted, 'diagnostics': diagnostics}\n```\n\nBut need to consider that the diagnostics list should be per call, not per accepted. That's fine.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they want diagnostics for accepted only? The phrase \"Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" Could mean diagnostics for each accepted call. But \"per-call diagnostics\" could mean for each call in the original list. I think full list is more likely because they said \"Keep diagnostics in supplied call order\", implying all calls. So we output diagnostics for all calls in order.\n\nI'll proceed with that.\n\nNow put into JSON string.\n\nI'll produce the JSON object: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    accepted = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    for call in calls:\\n        gain = call['loss_without_call'] - call['loss_with_call']\\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\\n        normalized_gain = gain / max(tokens, 1)\\n        diagnostics.append(round(normalized_gain, 6))\\n        if call['parseable'] and normalized_gain >= threshold:\\n            accepted.append(call['call_id'])\\n    return {'accepted': accepted, 'diagnostics': diagnostics}\"}\n\nMake sure to escape backslashes and quotes properly. There are no backslashes except in newlines, which are escaped as \\n. Also single quotes are fine inside double-quoted JSON string. But we need to escape double quotes if any. There are none. Also need to escape newlines: each \\n is a literal newline character in the string. In JSON, we need to represent it as \\n. So the string will contain \\n sequences.\n\nLet me write it carefully.\n\nFinal JSON object:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    accepted = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    for call in calls:\\n        gain = call['loss_without_call'] - call['loss_with_call']\\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\\n        normalized_gain = gain / max(tokens, 1)\\n        diagnostics.append(round(normalized_gain, 6))\\n        if call['parseable'] and normalized_gain >= threshold:\\n            accepte